;; Genesis 2. Format: gsb/translation/README.md. Check: make gsb/lint

(note "**The divine name.** From 2:4 the Greek alternates between ὁ θεός, \"God\", and Κύριος ὁ θεός, \"the Lord God\". Κύριος without the article is the Septuagint's rendering of the divine name. Κύριος ὁ θεός is one segment, \"the Lord God\": the Greek article (before θεός) carries the English \"the\", moved to the front as English requires. **Differs from the Hebrew.** The Hebrew text has \"the LORD God\" (YHWH Elohim) throughout 2:4-3:23, but the Greek has plain ὁ θεός, \"God\", at 2:5, 7, 9, 19 and 21. The English follows the Greek in each verse. (From the translator's knowledge of the Hebrew.)")

(note "**ἄνθρωπος and Ἀδάμ.** The Greek says ὁ ἄνθρωπος, \"the human\", through 2:15, and Ἀδάμ, \"Adam\", from 2:16 on, while the Hebrew says ha-adam, \"the man\", in nearly all these places. We translate the Greek word in each place: ἄνθρωπος is always \"human\" (readable \"man\"), Ἀδάμ always \"Adam\". The Greek's article with the name (τῷ Ἀδάμ, τὸν Ἀδάμ) is aligned and left unrendered, as with \"God\".")

(note "**Paradise.** The Septuagint calls the garden παράδεισος, a park or orchard. Both layers keep \"paradise\", so that the New Testament's paradise (Luke 23:43; 2 Corinthians 12:4; Revelation 2:7, \"the tree of the life which is in the paradise of God\") is visibly the same word. \"Garden\" is kept for κῆπος (John 18:1).")

